A||||7||Richard Parnell||How does swaybar work?||||5||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||08-31-2000||01:54 PM||||I'm curious as to just how the rear sway bar actaully stabilizes the ride of the car.  It looks like it just bolts from one side of the cradle to the other. It seems that all that would do is tighten up the cradle structure.  How does it help with cornering.  I can't figure it.||||reg||5
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||08-31-2000||02:16 PM||||if memory serves me correct, I beleive it actually connects to the control arms, so that basically when one wheel begins pulling or the car lists to one side, it transfers some of that energy to the other side.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||08-31-2000||03:04 PM||||TRG is right.  The sway (actually anti-sway) bar pivots in brackets mounted to the cradle/frame.  The ends are attached to the A-Arms via end links.  As the suspension is compressed on one side the sway bar, under torsional load,  attempts to compress the other side the same amount.  This aids in keeping the body level to the pavement during cornering which helps keep the maximum size contact patch between the tire and the road surface.<P>Another effect is that an anti-sway bar increases the spring rate of the side being compressed when the bar twists.  For example, when one wheel goes over a bump the torsional effect of the anti-sway bar is added to the spring rate of that side.  This would make the bump feel more harsh than if the anti-sway bar was not connected.<P>I installed the Addco bar from JC Whitney to my 85SE v-6 and am very pleased with the outcome.  The car corners much better and is more exciting to drive (closer to neutral steer).  I picked up a front anti-sway bar from an 85SE at the junk yard for $10.00 and plan to install it on the back of my 85GT per Randy Agee's write-up.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000003||mshill||08-31-2000||04:14 PM||||Carrolles,  is there a pointer to Randy's write up?||||reg||1
